    "slug": "youtube-shorts-seo",
    "title": "YouTube Shorts SEO: How to Actually Get Views on Shorts in 2026",
    "description": "Most Shorts creators ignore SEO completely. Here is how the Shorts algorithm actually works and what you can do to get your short-form content seen by thousands.",
    "content": "**Written by Shiva | Lead Developer, FreeViralKit** ## Let's Talk About the Elephant in the Room Shorts are weird. They play by completely different rules than regular YouTube videos, and most creators pretend the same strategies work for both. They don't. I've watched creators with 500K subscribers post Shorts that get 200 views. I've also seen brand new channels post a single Short that hits 2 million. The Shorts algorithm is its own beast, and if you want to win, you need to understand how it thinks. *(Shorts require an entirely different approach to SEO).* ## The Shorts Algorithm Is NOT the YouTube Algorithm This is the single biggest misconception. Regular YouTube videos rely heavily on **watch time**, **click-through rate**, and **session duration**. The Shorts algorithm asks a fundamentally different question: **\"Did people swipe past this, or did they watch it?\"** That's it. The core metric is **swipe-away rate** - the percentage of people who see your Short and swipe to the next one within the first 1-2 seconds. If most people swipe away, your Short dies. If most people stay, YouTube pushes it. ## The First Second Is Everything I'm not exaggerating. You have roughly **one second** to convince someone not to swipe. That's not enough time for an intro or a greeting. The best-performing Shorts do one of these things in the first frame: 1. **Start mid-action:** Don't show yourself walking to the kitchen. Start with the oil already sizzling in the pan.\n2. **Lead with a bold statement:** \"Most people do this completely wrong\" immediately creates an information gap.\n3. **Show the result first:** The finished product, the final score, the transformation. Then explain how you got there.\n4. **Use text on screen:** A lot of Shorts viewers watch without sound. Put the hook as text overlay in the first frame. ## Titles Still Matter (But Differently) Here's something that surprises people: Shorts titles ARE indexed by YouTube search. But here's the difference: **nobody sees your title in the Shorts feed.** When someone is scrolling through Shorts, they only see the video itself. Your title only matters for search discovery and the Shorts shelf on your channel page. Write titles that are keyword-rich for search, but don't stress about making them \"clickable.\" Keep titles under 40 characters when possible, as they get truncated aggressively. ## Hashtags Work Differently for Shorts For regular videos, hashtags are a minor discovery boost. For Shorts, they actually matter more because the algorithm uses them to categorize your content and decide which audience to test it with. Here is my exact approach:\n* **Always include #Shorts.** Shorts with this hashtag still get picked up by the shelf more reliably.\n* **Use 3-5 niche hashtags.** Pick the most specific ones so the algorithm understands your content quickly.\n* **Put them in the description, not the title.** Hashtags in the title eat into your limited character count. ## The Posting Strategy Nobody Talks About Volume matters for Shorts way more than it does for long-form. The algorithm tests each Short with a small initial audience. If it performs well, it gets pushed. If it doesn't, it stops. You can't predict which Short will take off. I've seen creators spend 3 hours on a perfectly edited Short that gets 500 views, then post a quick 15-second clip that took 10 minutes to make and it hits 100K. **My Verdict:** Post 4-7 Shorts per week minimum. Each Short is a lottery ticket; the more tickets you have, the better your odds. ## Automate Your Shorts Metadata Shorts SEO is simpler than long-form SEO, but it's not optional. The creators who optimize their titles, hashtags, and tags consistently outperform the ones who just upload and hope for the best. Don't spend hours on metadata for a 30-second video. Use our [YouTube Title Generator for Vlogs](/tools/youtube-title-generator-for-vlogs) or our [YouTube Tags Generator](/youtube-tags-generator) to get optimized metadata in seconds.",
